:sdx_status_plug:
> **What this plugin is.** A kernel driver (module `synology_dx`) for Synology expansion units built on the Marvell 88SM9705 port multiplier: tested on the DX1215 (RX1214 and RX413 use the same chip, untested). It talks to the unit's control chip over the SATA cable and gives the Synology Hardware plugin the fan level, both fan alarms, power and PSU status and the unit model. It changes nothing by itself: the fan level is set by the Synology Hardware plugin (or by hand in `/sys/kernel/synology_dx/unit/fan_level`: full, middle, low).
>
> **The checks.** At every boot and plugin install the loader checks that the plugin has a driver build for the running Unraid kernel, that the file is intact (sha256) and made for this kernel (vermagic), finds the unit's port multipliers in sysfs, takes the address of the kernel function the driver needs (`ata_exec_internal`) from `/proc/kallsyms`, and loads the driver. The driver checks the address and each port again before it does anything, and refuses on any mismatch. It then reads the unit's status lines and puts every register it touched back as it found it. The loader's record: `/var/run/synology-expansion/checks`; syslog tag `synology-expansion`.
>
> **The findings** come from the unit master (the port multiplier with EMID 0). Bit meanings are from Synology's own code (`research/2026-10-25-dx1215-gpio-spec.md`) and were measured on this unit (`experiments/2026-10-25-synology-dx-phase2a/RESULT.md`, `-phase2b/RESULT.md`). Values not yet confirmed are shown raw and marked.
>
> **Bay LEDs** (2026.10.26a). On by default. Green = presence/activity, driven by the controllers themselves. Orange = fault: the Synology Hardware plugin's daemon (which already owns the fan policy and reads Unraid's disk state) sets it from disk health, about once a minute; this plugin only switches the LEDs on and shows what is set. The bay table's Health column is that daemon's verdict per disk.
>
> **Cost of this page.** Opening it may make the driver re-read the unit (at most every 30 s): one short pause of the unit's master SATA port.
:end
